WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.900
Chapter 1: Getting Started with AI Agents.

2
00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:14.075
If you have used Cursor to write code and watched it search your codebase, edit multiple files, and rerun tests until they pass, you have already used an AI Agent.

3
00:00:14.075 --> 00:00:30.500
The same is true if you have used Deep Research to investigate a topic through repeated searching and reading, had Manus control a browser to finish online tasks, asked the Doubao phone assistant to book tickets or send messages, or sent Pine AI to negotiate a lower telecom bill.

4
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:39.450
These products take many forms, but they share a common trait: they are no longer passive "you ask, it answers" conversations.

5
00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:47.375
They plan their own execution steps, call the tools each task requires, and adjust their strategy as results come in.

6
00:00:47.375 --> 00:00:51.787
AI Agents are becoming a new way to interact with computers.

7
00:00:51.787 --> 00:01:07.725
This chapter begins with practical examples and works back toward the core components of an AI Agent: readers will experience firsthand what modern Agents can do, understand the architecture behind them, and learn the design patterns and best practices for building Agent systems.

8
00:01:07.725 --> 00:01:18.850
Reading Tip: This chapter is the conceptual map for the whole book: a concise tour of the core formula, operating loop, engineering framework, and Agent design patterns.

9
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:24.537
It establishes the shared vocabulary and reference points used throughout later chapters.

10
00:01:24.537 --> 00:01:29.925
Do not try to memorize every concept on your first read; aim for the big picture.

11
00:01:29.925 --> 00:01:37.237
Each later chapter expands on one aspect introduced here, and you can return to this chapter whenever you need to reorient.

12
00:01:37.237 --> 00:01:41.637
Modern Agent = LLM + Context + Tools.

13
00:01:41.637 --> 00:01:50.987
At its simplest, a modern agent consists of three components: Agent = LLM (Large Language Model) + Context + Tools.

14
00:01:50.987 --> 00:02:05.712
The plus signs here denote a combination of engineering components, not a formal definition from reinforcement learning; more importantly, the formula describes only what lies inside the Agent's boundary and does not include the Environment the Agent interacts with.

15
00:02:05.712 --> 00:02:09.825
Each term must be read broadly, but with a clear boundary:

16
00:02:09.825 --> 00:02:22.262
The LLM is the Agent's brain: It is more than a set of model parameters; it is the Agent's entire decision-making core—responsible for understanding intent, reasoning, planning, and judgment.

17
00:02:22.262 --> 00:02:45.312
Just as the human brain is not merely a collection of neurons but also encompasses thought patterns sculpted through experience, an LLM's capabilities stem from two sources: world knowledge and linguistic proficiency acquired during pre-training, and decision policies solidified through post-training (specific techniques such as supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning are detailed in Chapter 8).

18
00:02:45.312 --> 00:03:02.125
Context is the Agent's eyes: It is not merely the text fed into the model, but the information representation received and retained by the Agent at each decision point—including observations from the environment, user memory, domain knowledge, internal state, and task progress.

19
00:03:02.125 --> 00:03:23.575
Tools are the Agent's hands and feet: Here, "tools" refers to the interfaces the Agent uses to perceive or transform the external world, encompassing tool definitions, calling protocols, and adapters—ranging from predefined tool invocations to dynamic code generation, and from delegating tasks to sub-agents to proactively communicating with users.

20
00:03:23.575 --> 00:03:28.850
Put more intuitively: Agent = Brain + Eyes + Hands and Feet.

21
00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:39.587
The brain handles reasoning and decisions, the eyes receive observations provided by the environment, and the hands and feet convert decisions into actions that operate upon the environment.

22
00:03:39.587 --> 00:03:49.462
From the classical reinforcement-learning and control perspective, the Agent and the Environment are two sides of a closed-loop interaction, not components of one another.

23
00:03:49.462 --> 00:03:59.787
The Environment returns an observation, the Agent uses its context to choose the next action, and that action changes the Environment's state, producing the next observation.

24
00:03:59.787 --> 00:04:08.012
As illustrated in Figure 1-1: The Agent–Environment interaction loop and the Model–Harness structure inside the Agent.

25
00:04:08.164 --> 00:04:12.139
Figure 1-1 shows two levels of abstraction.

26
00:04:12.089 --> 00:04:24.501
The outer level is the interaction between the Agent and the Environment: the Environment includes file systems, databases, web pages, users, other Agents, and physical or simulated worlds.

27
00:04:24.501 --> 00:04:42.789
The inner level is the Model–Harness structure inside the Agent: the Model makes policy decisions; the Harness is the runtime and governance layer inside the Agent boundary that builds context, exposes tool interfaces, maintains loops and state, and applies permissions, verification, and correction.

28
00:04:42.789 --> 00:04:50.201
A Harness can create, isolate, or proxy an environment without containing the Environment's state or transition rules.

29
00:04:50.201 --> 00:05:04.064
The engineering formula can therefore be expanded as follows: the LLM is the Model, while Context + Tools form the minimum Harness; production systems add constraints, verification, and correction inside that boundary.

30
00:05:04.064 --> 00:05:07.314
The rest of this chapter follows this boundary.

31
00:05:07.314 --> 00:05:17.214
These three components can be related to policies and interaction interfaces in RL (reinforcement learning; see Chapter 8), but they do not map one-to-one.

32
00:05:17.214 --> 00:05:24.139
Context represents observations and history inside the Agent; it is not the entire observation space.

33
00:05:24.139 --> 00:05:31.939
Tools define the observation and action interfaces available to the Agent, while the objects they act on remain in the Environment.

34
00:05:31.939 --> 00:05:47.376
Intuition: Brain; Agent Component: LLM; RL Concept: Policy; Role: The decision-making logic that determines "what to do next"—given the current observations, choose the most appropriate action from all available options.

35
00:05:47.376 --> 00:06:01.276
Intuition: Eyes; Agent Component: Context construction; RL Concept: Observations and history; Role: Organizes Environment observations and existing history into the information needed for the current decision.

36
00:06:01.276 --> 00:06:17.851
Intuition: Hands and Feet; Agent Component: Tool interfaces & adapters; RL Concept: Observation/action interfaces; Role: Defines which observations the Agent can read, which actions it can issue, and the format of those interfaces.

37
00:06:17.851 --> 00:06:23.001
Observation and Action Spaces: The Interface Between Model and World.

38
00:06:23.001 --> 00:06:30.176
Observation channels and action interfaces together constitute the boundary between the Agent and its external environment.

39
00:06:30.176 --> 00:06:40.789
The Harness translates observations returned by the environment into context the model can process, and converts the actions chosen by the model into tool calls against the environment.

40
00:06:40.789 --> 00:06:54.764
Information that does not enter context via observation channels effectively does not exist for the model; operations not permitted by action interfaces remain things the model can only recommend in words, even if it knows exactly what should be done.

41
00:06:54.764 --> 00:07:06.651
Consequently, once the underlying model is held constant, the primary systems-engineering lever for improving Agent performance is often to redefine or expand its observation and action spaces.

42
00:07:06.651 --> 00:07:11.776
In this book's terminology, that means expanding context and tools.

43
00:07:11.776 --> 00:07:25.614
Many problems that appear to require a “smarter model” are really interface problems: bring the task-relevant data into context or expose the required operation as a tool, and a previously unsolvable task may become solvable.

44
00:07:25.614 --> 00:07:29.189
Manus: merging spaces that had been separate.

45
00:07:29.189 --> 00:07:37.551
Before Manus appeared, production Agents mostly followed three distinct tracks: Deep Research, Coding, and Computer Use.

46
00:07:37.551 --> 00:07:43.664
Manus was the first widely influential production Agent to bring all three together in one system.

47
00:07:43.664 --> 00:07:53.164
Its virtual browser enlarged the observation space, while its file system, code execution, and command execution enlarged the action space.

48
00:07:53.164 --> 00:07:58.101
Manus did not become a general Agent merely by swapping in a stronger model.

49
00:07:58.101 --> 00:08:06.576
It took the union of three kinds of Agents' observation and action spaces, enabling one Agent to cross the previous product boundaries.

50
00:08:06.748 --> 00:08:11.385
OpenClaw: extending the interface into the user's digital life.

51
00:08:11.335 --> 00:08:14.873
OpenClaw pushes both spaces outward again.

52
00:08:14.873 --> 00:08:27.998
It receives tasks and returns results through messaging channels users already inhabit—WhatsApp, Telegram, Slack, Discord, iMessage, and many others—so the Agent can be reached from almost anywhere.

53
00:08:27.998 --> 00:08:34.823
Its local Gateway connects cloud applications such as Google Drive and Notion as well as the local file system.

54
00:08:34.823 --> 00:08:45.073
Files scattered across accounts and devices can therefore, with the user's explicit authorization, enter one Agent's observation space and be acted on by its tools.

55
00:08:45.073 --> 00:08:56.323
Compared with the original cloud-sandbox-centered form of Manus, where files generally had to be uploaded or a connector separately configured, local-first OpenClaw spans a broader data boundary.

56
00:08:56.323 --> 00:09:09.323
Manus later added its own Google Drive connector and desktop access to local files, which only reinforces the point: product evolution often consists precisely of expanding observation and action spaces.

57
00:09:09.323 --> 00:09:16.985
Understanding what each component does, and how they fit together, is the foundation for building effective Agent systems.

58
00:09:16.985 --> 00:09:25.273
We will begin with the most concrete of the three—tools, the hands and feet—and work inward to the LLM and context.

59
00:09:25.273 --> 00:09:30.573
First, here is how different kinds of Agents compare across these three dimensions:

60
00:09:30.573 --> 00:09:56.048
Agent Product: Coding Agents (e.g., Cursor); Eyes (Perception): Requirements documents, code snippets read, directory listings, terminal output; Hands and Feet (Action): Open-ended (code search, file read/write, command execution, etc.); Strategy: Incremental development: understand requirements → search relevant code → edit code → test and verify → debug and fix.

61
00:09:56.048 --> 00:10:20.523
Agent Product: Search Agents (e.g., Deep Research); Eyes (Perception): Search results, web content, paper abstracts and citations; Hands and Feet (Action): Open-ended (search queries, web reading, summary generation, etc.); Strategy: Iterative deepening: adjust search direction based on existing information, gradually synthesize a complete report.

62
00:10:20.523 --> 00:10:45.610
Agent Product: Computer Control Agents (e.g., Browser Use); Eyes (Perception): Computer screen, browser pages, file system; Hands and Feet (Action): Open-ended (clicking, typing, scrolling, screenshots, code execution, etc.); Strategy: Visual perception + operation: observe screen → identify target elements → perform actions → verify results.

63
00:10:45.610 --> 00:11:07.460
Agent Product: Phone Assistant Agents (e.g., Doubao); Eyes (Perception): Phone screen, installed apps; Hands and Feet (Action): Open-ended (clicking, swiping, typing, opening apps, etc.); Strategy: Intent understanding + App control: understand user needs → locate target app → perform actions → confirm completion.

64
00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:34.473
Agent Product: Personal Task Agents (e.g., Pine AI); Eyes (Perception): Authorized account records, historical bills, service provider materials; Hands and Feet (Action): Open-ended (making calls, sending emails, filling forms, confirming with user); Strategy: Multi-step task execution: gather information → formulate negotiation strategy → contact service provider → negotiate → report results.

65
00:11:34.473 --> 00:11:51.748
These systems share three features: an open-ended action space—not picking from a fixed set of buttons but generating arbitrary natural language and code; internal reasoning—planning before acting; and continuous interaction—adjusting strategy based on environmental feedback.

66
00:11:51.748 --> 00:12:01.548
These capabilities come precisely from the interplay of the brain, eyes, and hands and feet—that is, LLM, context, and tools.

67
00:12:01.548 --> 00:12:04.748
Tools: The Agent's Hands and Feet.

68
00:12:04.748 --> 00:12:16.323
Tools are the bridge for an Agent's interaction with the external world: perception tools carry observations from the environment to the Agent, while execution tools carry actions from the Agent to the environment.

69
00:12:16.323 --> 00:12:24.348
Without tools, an Agent is limited to paper exercises; with them, it can genuinely perceive or transform the world.

70
00:12:24.508 --> 00:12:31.858
To discuss tools systematically, we can sort them into five types by the direction of the Agent's interaction with the world.

71
00:12:31.808 --> 00:12:38.920
For now, a few examples of each type will give us the overall picture; later chapters explore them in depth.

72
00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:52.595
Perception Tools allow the Agent to access information: search engines provide real-time web data, file systems read local documents, and APIs and databases connect to external services and enterprise core data.

73
00:12:52.595 --> 00:13:04.895
Execution Tools allow the Agent to act on external systems: code execution, file operations, system commands, and external API calls turn decisions into concrete actions.

74
00:13:04.895 --> 00:13:18.045
Collaboration Tools allow the Agent to divide work with other Agents: delegating specialized tasks to sub-agents, requesting human confirmation at key decision points, or coordinating actions in multi-agent systems.

75
00:13:18.045 --> 00:13:28.933
Event Trigger Tools are invoked in a fundamentally different way from the first three categories: the Agent does not call them; they arrive as external inputs that trigger the Agent to begin work.

76
00:13:28.933 --> 00:13:39.083
A new email comes in, a scheduled time arrives, or another system fires a Webhook callback; the event activates the Agent and initiates reasoning and action.

77
00:13:39.083 --> 00:13:47.758
The Agent never calls these itself, yet they are still a channel through which it interacts with the outside world, so we count them in the broad tool system.

78
00:13:47.758 --> 00:13:53.145
User Communication Tools are the channels through which the Agent communicates with the user.

79
00:13:53.145 --> 00:14:05.995
Where execution tools change the external world, communication tools carry information—delivering the Agent's progress, or a proactive check-in, by text message, voice call, email, and so on.

80
00:14:05.995 --> 00:14:11.533
Chapter 4 covers the full taxonomy and design principles for these five types.

81
00:14:11.533 --> 00:14:28.270
The quality of tool design directly determines what an Agent can reliably accomplish: define interfaces vaguely and the model will misuse them; handle errors poorly and a single failed tool can leave the Agent stuck; scope permissions too broadly and one Agent error can become irreversible.

82
00:14:28.270 --> 00:14:35.708
As the MCP (Model Context Protocol) standard spreads, integrating tools is becoming easier.

83
00:14:35.708 --> 00:14:51.795
Tool Calling (also known as Function Calling) is a core capability of modern LLM Agents: it lets the model invoke external tools in a structured way, transforming the LLM from a pure text generator into an intelligent system that can act through external interfaces.

84
00:14:51.795 --> 00:14:55.645
This book uses the term "tool calling" throughout.

85
00:14:55.645 --> 00:15:18.395
Tool calling proceeds in four steps: first, the context tells the model which tools are available (names, purposes, parameters); then the model decides on its own whether to call a tool, which tool to call, and with what arguments; next, once the tool has run, its result is appended to the context; finally, the model decides its next move based on that result.

86
00:15:18.395 --> 00:15:23.183
This loop is the foundation of ReAct, introduced later in the chapter.

87
00:15:23.183 --> 00:15:30.170
For a weather query, the simplified representation of the four-step process at the API level is as follows:

88
00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:37.215
Here is the four-step tool invocation process: Step 1, declare tools in the system context.

89
00:15:37.165 --> 00:15:43.177
Step 2, the model decides to call a tool and outputs the function name and arguments.

90
00:15:43.177 --> 00:15:47.602
Step 3, the Harness executes the tool in the environment.

91
00:15:47.602 --> 00:15:54.902
Step 4, the Harness feeds the tool result back into context, and the model synthesizes the final answer.

92
00:15:54.902 --> 00:16:04.540
The developer only defines the tools and executes the calls; the model itself decides whether to call, which tool to call, and what arguments to pass.

93
00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:08.640
Chapter 2 examines this API structure in detail.

94
00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:17.577
When designing tools for an Agent, start with the narrowest capability the task needs, then expand gradually as the task grows more complex.

95
00:16:17.577 --> 00:16:37.352
If the task only requires basic arithmetic, a calculator with clearly defined parameters is enough; when it grows to reading spreadsheets, cleaning missing values, computing statistics, and plotting charts, a constrained Python code interpreter is easier to combine and explore with than an ever-growing collection of specialized tools.

96
00:16:37.352 --> 00:16:56.402
But generality also increases the risk of errors and expands the attack surface: code must run in an isolated sandbox, with network access disabled by default, no access to files outside the authorized working directory, and limits on execution time, CPU, memory, and output size.

97
00:16:56.402 --> 00:17:14.415
Likewise, a single logging tool is suitable for recording one execution; for long-running tasks that take hours or even days, a controlled virtual working directory can preserve plans, intermediate results, execution logs, and final artifacts so the Agent can resume across multiple runs.

98
00:17:14.415 --> 00:17:26.252
This directory should also restrict readable and writable paths, storage capacity, and file types, and prevent path traversal instead of exposing the entire host file system to the Agent.

99
00:17:26.252 --> 00:17:30.727
General-purpose tools are not always better than specialized ones.

100
00:17:30.727 --> 00:17:50.627
High-risk operations or those governed by strict business constraints—such as payments, data deletion, sending email, and production deployment—should still be exposed as dedicated tools with explicit parameters, restricted permissions, and end-to-end auditability, with previews and human confirmation added when necessary.

101
00:17:50.627 --> 00:18:03.677
The core principle of tool design is therefore: use general-purpose foundational capabilities for composition and exploration; use specialized tools to constrain high-risk operations and enforce strict business rules.

102
00:18:03.677 --> 00:18:06.452
LLM: The Agent's Brain.

103
00:18:06.452 --> 00:18:11.602
The Large Language Model (LLM) is the Agent's decision-making core.

104
00:18:11.602 --> 00:18:22.902
Given a user request, it first has to infer the real intent (what users say is often not what they actually want), then break a vague or complex task into executable steps.

105
00:18:22.902 --> 00:18:31.240
Throughout execution it keeps making decisions: what to do next, whether to call a tool, which one, and with what arguments.

106
00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:41.302
This understand–plan–execute capability comes from knowledge accumulated during pre-training, and it is the foundation that workflows and autonomous Agents alike depend on.

107
00:18:41.302 --> 00:18:49.540
A distinctive capability of LLM Agents is internal reasoning—before acting, the Agent can plan and reason through the task.

108
00:18:49.540 --> 00:18:55.427
This does not change the external environment, yet it markedly improves the actions that follow.

109
00:18:55.427 --> 00:19:13.615
This ability comes from pre-training (the initial training on massive amounts of internet text, through which the model learns language patterns and world knowledge): the model draws on reasoning patterns encoded in human knowledge, including mathematical laws, causal relationships, and strategies for decomposing problems.

110
00:19:13.615 --> 00:19:24.577
Therefore, unlike traditional reinforcement-learning Agents, today's LLM-based Agents do not explore through blind trial and error; they reason over a structured body of knowledge.

111
00:19:24.577 --> 00:19:28.940
Model as Agent: When the Model Itself Becomes the Product.

112
00:19:29.092 --> 00:19:34.767
The "Model as Agent" paradigm is the newest direction in AI Agent development.

113
00:19:34.717 --> 00:19:48.967
Advanced models internalize tool calling as a native ability through post-training (especially reinforcement learning): when to call a tool, which one, with what arguments—the model decides all of it, with no manual orchestration required.

114
00:19:48.967 --> 00:19:52.604
That does not make the framework layer less important.

115
00:19:52.604 --> 00:19:57.954
On the contrary: the stronger the model, the more the surrounding Harness matters.

116
00:19:57.954 --> 00:20:06.804
The word Harness originally referred to the reins and tack fitted to a horse—not to limit its ability to run, but to direct that power appropriately.

117
00:20:06.804 --> 00:20:17.767
In the Agent context, the model is the powerful yet unpredictable horse, while the Harness is the engineering infrastructure that channels its capability into reliable task execution.

118
00:20:17.767 --> 00:20:27.429
It includes context management, tool interfaces, safety constraints, and verification and correction mechanisms (see the final section of this chapter).

119
00:20:27.429 --> 00:20:38.079
The more decision authority a model has, the greater the impact of a wrong decision—which calls for finer-grained constraint, verification, and correction to keep it reliable.

120
00:20:38.079 --> 00:20:47.929
The real advantage of model providers is not "making the framework thinner" but being able to co-optimize the model and its surrounding Harness, iterating continuously.

121
00:20:47.929 --> 00:20:55.367
But a deeper question follows: if models keep getting stronger, will today's Harness eventually be absorbed into the model?

122
00:20:55.367 --> 00:21:02.192
In “The Bitter Lesson,” Rich Sutton looked back on a pattern repeated throughout seventy years of AI research

123
00:21:02.192 --> 00:21:07.704
Agent Learning Mechanisms: From Contextual Adaptation to Persistent Updates.

124
00:21:07.704 --> 00:21:15.317
The preceding discussion noted that a model can internalize tool-use policies as native capabilities through reinforcement learning.

125
00:21:15.317 --> 00:21:19.929
But changes in an Agent's behavior do not occur only during training.

126
00:21:19.929 --> 00:21:35.592
Based on where an update occurs and how long it persists, these changes can be understood as three complementary paths (Figure 1-2): within-task contextual adaptation, cross-task updates to external artifacts, and parameter updates during training cycles.

127
00:21:35.592 --> 00:21:41.367
As illustrated in Figure 1-2: Three Levels of Agent Capability Updates.

128
00:21:41.367 --> 00:21:45.429
Contextual adaptation occurs within the current task.

129
00:21:45.429 --> 00:21:56.004
Once examples, state, and retrieval results enter the context, the model can adjust its behavior immediately, but this does not change the persistent state of the next session.

130
00:21:56.004 --> 00:22:03.817
Its advantages are speed and low cost; its limitations arise from the context window and the way information is organized.

131
00:22:03.817 --> 00:22:08.554
Chapter 2 explains in detail how this form of adaptation works.

132
00:22:08.554 --> 00:22:26.017
For changes to persist across tasks, the system can update external artifacts: facts and experience can be organized into knowledge documents, strategies expressible in language can be written into a Prompt or Skill, and deterministic procedures and constraints can be encoded in programs and Harnesses.

133
00:22:26.017 --> 00:22:34.542
These artifacts are auditable and revisable, but the Agent must still access them at execution time through the context or tool interfaces.

134
00:22:34.542 --> 00:22:45.442
Chapters 3 through 5 establish the foundations for knowledge and programs, while Chapter 9 discusses how such updates can be generated from evaluated operational trajectories.

135
00:22:45.442 --> 00:22:59.917
When the objective is a high-dimensional capability—such as medical-image understanding, natural-language style, or an implicit decision policy—that external rules cannot fully express, model parameters must be updated through post-training.

136
00:22:59.917 --> 00:23:08.804
Parameter updates carry higher deployment costs but can produce natural and broad generalization; Chapter 8 presents their methods systematically.

137
00:23:08.804 --> 00:23:25.854
The three paths are therefore not mutually exclusive categories but coordinated mechanisms operating at different timescales: context supports immediate adaptation, external artifacts support controlled accumulation, and parameters internalize capabilities that are difficult to express explicitly.

138
00:23:25.854 --> 00:23:28.767
Context: The Agent's Eyes.

139
00:23:28.924 --> 00:23:34.386
Context is the working set of information available to an Agent at each decision point.

140
00:23:34.336 --> 00:23:47.399
Just as a person making a decision needs the right materials on the table—task instructions, reference manuals, earlier correspondence, the latest data—an Agent's context window is the information it can use.

141
00:23:47.399 --> 00:23:55.386
From the API's perspective (detailed in Chapter 2), the context of each LLM call consists of five parts:

142
00:23:55.386 --> 00:24:04.624
System Prompt: Unlike the prompts users enter during a conversation, the system prompt is written by the developer and stays fixed for the whole conversation.

143
00:24:04.624 --> 00:24:11.349
It is the Agent’s “job description”—defining its identity, permissions, and rules of conduct.

144
00:24:11.349 --> 00:24:16.961
Careful prompt engineering of the system prompt is how we shape the Agent’s operating behavior.

145
00:24:16.961 --> 00:24:31.161
The system prompt also carries user memory that persists across sessions (personalized information such as preferences, past behavior, and background settings; see Chapter 3), plus dynamically injected environmental state.

146
00:24:31.161 --> 00:24:39.161
Tool Definitions: Declares the names, functional descriptions, and parameter formats of the tools available to the Agent.

147
00:24:39.161 --> 00:24:50.986
Without tool definitions, the Agent cannot recognize or call any tools—though it does not fall silent either—an ablation study (Experiment 1-1) will show what it does instead.

148
00:24:50.986 --> 00:24:58.836
Tool definitions, together with the system prompt, form the static prefix that remains unchanged throughout the conversation.

149
00:24:58.836 --> 00:25:12.836
This is the foundational pattern; since 2026, production frameworks can also load full tool schemas on demand at the end of the context without breaking the prefix—see the tool definitions section of Chapter 2 and Chapter 4.

150
00:25:12.836 --> 00:25:16.136
User Messages: Input from the user.

151
00:25:16.136 --> 00:25:30.111
User messages may also contain external knowledge dynamically retrieved via RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation, see Chapter 3 for details)—covering information beyond the training data cutoff or private domain knowledge.

152
00:25:30.111 --> 00:25:47.861
Assistant Messages: Responses previously generated by the model, which can contain up to three parts—reasoning (the internal chain of thought, maintaining coherence and decision interpretability), content (the response to the user), and tool_calls (the way the Agent takes action).

153
00:25:47.861 --> 00:26:04.061
In a specific response, these three parts may not all appear simultaneously: for example, when the Agent decides to call a tool, it usually only has reasoning + tool_calls; when giving a final answer, it usually only has reasoning + content.

154
00:26:04.061 --> 00:26:09.549
Tool Results: The output returned after the Agent framework executes a tool.

155
00:26:09.549 --> 00:26:17.586
These results are the direct basis for the Agent’s next reasoning step—and what lets it learn from outcomes rather than repeat its mistakes.

156
00:26:17.586 --> 00:26:31.861
The first two items (system prompt + tool definitions) form the static prefix; the last three (user messages + assistant messages + tool results) form the dynamic message history that grows with every interaction.

157
00:26:31.861 --> 00:26:36.999
Together, these five parts make up the context of each LLM inference.

158
00:26:36.999 --> 00:26:40.161
Is every component truly indispensable?

159
00:26:40.161 --> 00:26:55.399
The most direct way to find out is an ablation study—the diagnostic method of ruling out causes one at a time: remove component A and see whether the system still works, then component B, and so on, until each component's contribution is clear.

160
00:26:55.399 --> 00:27:00.711
Experiment 1-1 applies exactly this method to the five components above.

161
00:27:00.711 --> 00:27:07.224
Experiment 1-1 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : The Critical Role of Context

162
00:27:07.224 --> 00:27:13.274
We probed how each context component shapes Agent behavior with a systematic ablation study.

163
00:27:13.274 --> 00:27:26.299
Of the five components above, four were tested—the system prompt, as the Agent’s basic identity definition, was exempt: without it the Agent has no role awareness at all, and the test would be meaningless.

164
00:27:26.299 --> 00:27:39.599
As Figure 1-3 shows, the experiment ran five controlled groups: a complete baseline retaining every component, plus four groups each missing one, to observe each component’s effect on Agent performance.

165
00:27:39.599 --> 00:27:46.674
As illustrated in Figure 1-3: Experiment 1-1—Context ablation study design.

166
00:27:46.828 --> 00:27:53.465
The experimental results revealed the role of each context component—and that they are not equally important.

167
00:27:53.415 --> 00:28:02.940
Tool Definitions (part of the static prefix) are the foundation of the Agent’s action capability; without them, the Agent cannot call any tool.

168
00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:18.240
But losing the ability to act does not mean falling silent: the model still returns a neatly formatted, confidently worded answer whose numbers come from parametric memory rather than from observations, laid out exactly like an answer genuinely derived from tool output.

169
00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:32.128
Whether it declines outright or fabricates on the spot depends mainly on the model’s own hallucination rate and honesty; a constraint in the prompt such as “do not estimate the rates yourself” lowers the odds of fabrication without eliminating it.

170
00:28:32.128 --> 00:28:40.865
Tool Results are key to closed-loop control; without them the Agent executes “blind,” retrying until it exhausts its iteration budget.

171
00:28:40.865 --> 00:28:54.565
The reasoning process (the reasoning part of assistant messages) records why a step was taken, while tool results record what happened; when the former can be reconstructed from the latter, dropping it from the history costs almost nothing.

172
00:28:54.565 --> 00:29:05.203
Message history (user messages, assistant messages, and tool results from previous rounds) prevents redundant operations and avoids repeating the same mistakes.

173
00:29:05.203 --> 00:29:13.328
The experiment's core insight: context determines what the Agent can see, and the Agent can only decide based on what it sees.

174
00:29:13.328 --> 00:29:30.628
But the components are not equivalent; the measure is whether the information one carries can be reconstructed from somewhere else—claims like “every component is indispensable” have to be measured rather than assumed, and models move fast enough that the same ablation on a newer model may well reach a different conclusion.

175
00:29:30.628 --> 00:29:37.678
One more point matters more in engineering practice: “produced an answer” is not “completed the task.

176
00:29:37.678 --> 00:29:44.640
When a context component is missing, the typical failure is not an error exit but an answer that looks flawless.

177
00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:46.515
The ReAct Loop.

178
00:29:46.515 --> 00:29:52.178
With the three components in hand, a natural question follows: how do they work together?

179
00:29:52.178 --> 00:29:58.678
The ReAct loop is the core mechanism that connects LLM, context, and tools into one system.

180
00:29:58.678 --> 00:30:01.465
We can examine it step by step.

181
00:30:01.465 --> 00:30:07.265
The core pattern by which an Agent executes a task is called ReAct (Reasoning + Acting).

182
00:30:07.265 --> 00:30:20.628
The name mentions only reasoning and acting, but the actual loop has three stages: the model first reasons about what to do next, then calls a tool to act, then observes the tool’s result and reasons about the subsequent step.

183
00:30:20.628 --> 00:30:26.340
This “reason → act → observe → reason → act → observe” loop repeats until the task is done.

184
00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:43.375
Consider a concrete example—aggregating revenue across multiple currencies—to understand an Agent’s trajectory: the message history that accumulates as the Agent works, comprising user messages, assistant messages (with their reasoning and tool calls), and tool results.

185
00:30:43.325 --> 00:30:56.100
On every LLM call, the complete context the model receives is the static prefix (system prompt + tool definitions) plus the trajectory (dynamic message history) (Figure 1-4).

186
00:30:56.100 --> 00:31:01.650
This shows a key fact: Agent context = static prefix + trajectory.

187
00:31:01.650 --> 00:31:16.412
Concretely, the static prefix is the first two of the five components above (system prompt + tool definitions); the trajectory is the last three (user messages + assistant messages + tool results, growing with each interaction).

188
00:31:16.412 --> 00:31:24.162
From this complete context the LLM generates its next response, which is then appended to the trajectory for the subsequent call.

189
00:31:24.162 --> 00:31:31.487
As illustrated in Figure 1-4: Agent trajectory—ReAct loop for a multi-currency aggregation task.

190
00:31:31.487 --> 00:31:34.762
Start with a minimal implementation of the loop.

191
00:31:34.762 --> 00:31:48.000
It shows how the components work together: the Model only decides the next step, the Harness assembles the context and validates and executes the tools, and the Environment produces the actual state changes and observations.

192
00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:56.887
The rest of this book also uses Python-style pseudocode; the pseudocode is not runnable and does not correspond to any particular SDK.

193
00:31:56.887 --> 00:32:01.762
The concrete executable code lives in this book's companion repository.

194
00:32:01.762 --> 00:32:08.912
In pseudocode, the interaction loop proceeds as follows: Initialize the trajectory with the user request.

195
00:32:08.912 --> 00:32:15.487
In each iteration, construct the context, query the model, and append the decision to the trajectory.

196
00:32:15.487 --> 00:32:19.012
If no tool is called, return the answer.

197
00:32:19.012 --> 00:32:26.112
Otherwise, execute each tool call, append the observations, and repeat until the task is complete.

198
00:32:26.112 --> 00:32:29.962
Here is the structure of a trajectory, in pseudocode:

199
00:32:29.962 --> 00:32:38.150
Consider a concrete trajectory: The user asks to calculate total and average revenue across four quarters in different currencies.

200
00:32:38.150 --> 00:32:44.825
In the first iteration, the model calls exchange rate tools for Euros, Pounds, and Yen.

201
00:32:44.825 --> 00:32:49.312
In the second iteration, the Harness returns the exchange rates.

202
00:32:49.312 --> 00:33:01.512
In the third iteration, the model uses Python code to compute the totals in US dollars, obtaining 7.15 million dollars total and 1.79 million dollars average.

203
00:33:01.512 --> 00:33:05.312
Finally, the model outputs the verified answer.

204
00:33:05.312 --> 00:33:15.925
Note that the system prompt and tool definitions are not shown in the trajectory—they serve as the static prefix and are automatically prepended to the trajectory before each LLM call.

205
00:33:15.925 --> 00:33:19.762
In our experiment, this loop was clearly visible.

206
00:33:19.762 --> 00:33:37.987
In the first round, the Agent analyzed the task and called three currency conversion tools in parallel; in the second, it fed the conversion results to a code interpreter for the more computationally intensive calculation; in the third, having confirmed all calculations were complete, it produced the final answer.

207
00:33:37.987 --> 00:33:43.887
A complex multi-step task was completed in 3 iterations and 4 tool calls.

208
00:33:43.887 --> 00:33:50.025
In this most basic design, the context seen by the LLM is continually appended to.

209
00:33:50.025 --> 00:33:59.687
Every LLM call receives the complete trajectory, so the model knows which stage of the task it is in, what was tried before, and what the outcome was.

210
00:33:59.687 --> 00:34:07.737
Just as people keep reviewing and summarizing while solving a problem, the Agent maintains a global view of the task through its trajectory.

211
00:34:07.737 --> 00:34:19.937
And because the trajectory is structured—user messages, assistant messages (reasoning + tool calls), and tool results all separated cleanly—the system is highly interpretable and debuggable.

212
00:34:19.937 --> 00:34:26.850
Now that we understand the Agent's operating loop, we examine two experiments to see how different models drive it.

213
00:34:26.850 --> 00:34:34.012
Experiment 1-2 introductory difficulty, one star: : Kimi K3 Native Agent Capability

214
00:34:34.180 --> 00:34:42.242
This experiment demonstrates the native Agent capability of Kimi K3, an example of the “Model as Agent” paradigm.

215
00:34:42.192 --> 00:34:49.380
Kimi K3 is a Mixture of Experts (MoE) model with approximately 2.8 trillion parameters.

216
00:34:49.380 --> 00:35:02.092
MoE can be viewed as a team of experts: for each kind of problem, the system activates only the few experts best suited to it rather than the entire model, preserving capability without paying the full efficiency cost.

217
00:35:02.092 --> 00:35:09.955
Kimi K3 has a 1 million token context window, native visual understanding, and an always-on “thinking mode.

218
00:35:09.955 --> 00:35:25.667
Through reinforcement learning, it has internalized the tool-calling decision policy as a native capability: when to call a tool, which tool to call, and what arguments to pass are all decided by the model, allowing it to carry out tasks such as web searches autonomously.

219
00:35:25.667 --> 00:35:38.442
To be precise, what is internalized is the when and how to call decision; the tools themselves, such as web_search and code_runner, still execute server-side as API-level built-in tools.

220
00:35:38.442 --> 00:35:43.617
Kimi runs these official tools through a server-side script engine called Formula.

221
00:35:43.617 --> 00:35:52.567
The orchestration loop has therefore not disappeared; only the decision-making moved into the model, and where the loop itself runs depends on how you integrate.

222
00:35:52.567 --> 00:36:18.255
On the Responses API path of Experiment 1-3, search, code execution and the multi-round orchestration are closed on the server; on the Kimi path of Experiment 1-2, Formula only executes the search itself on the server, while the "call the model → append the tool result → call again" ReAct loop is still driven by client code (see the while loop in chapter1/web-search-agent/agent.py).

223
00:36:18.255 --> 00:36:29.830
The key observations are that the model decides when to search and what to search for, showing genuine autonomy; it adjusts strategy as search results arrive and judges whether it has enough information.

224
00:36:29.830 --> 00:36:38.017
A common misconception is worth clarifying: reinforcement learning gives the model the decision policy, not the tools themselves.

225
00:36:38.017 --> 00:36:53.230
It teaches when to call a tool, which tool to choose, what arguments to pass, whether to continue after receiving a result, and how to chain dozens or hundreds of calls into coherent reasoning; these whether-and-how-to-use judgments are what get written into the model's weights.

226
00:36:53.230 --> 00:37:08.342
The tools and their execution are provided by the Agent framework or API built-ins: the implementations of web_search and code_runner, the code sandbox, and the infrastructure that issues calls and returns results all live outside the model.

227
00:37:08.342 --> 00:37:15.917
RL optimizes the decision policy; it does not embed a search engine or a code sandbox into the model's weights.

228
00:37:15.917 --> 00:37:24.080
Thus, the orchestration loop has not disappeared; it has moved from the client to the server, while decision-making has moved into the model.

229
00:37:24.080 --> 00:37:28.530
Harness Engineering: Building Reliable Systems Around the Model.

230
00:37:28.530 --> 00:37:38.017
By now you understand how an Agent works at its core: an LLM runs the ReAct loop, guided by context, using tools to complete the task.

231
00:37:38.017 --> 00:37:44.542
The experiments above show that the basic mechanism works—and also expose how fragile it is.

232
00:37:44.542 --> 00:37:52.980
The model may hallucinate (invent tools or parameters that do not exist), pick the wrong tool, or fail to recover from an error.

233
00:37:52.980 --> 00:38:01.805
Between a working demo and a reliable product lies a substantial gap, and those fragilities are exactly what Harness Engineering exists to fix.

234
00:38:01.805 --> 00:38:09.267
The first half of this chapter answered what an Agent is; the second half answers how an Agent runs reliably in production.

235
00:38:09.436 --> 00:38:16.323
The preceding sections established the core formula: Agent = LLM + Context + Tools.

236
00:38:16.273 --> 00:38:23.611
It describes the Agent's internal composition: reasoning engine, working context, and action interfaces.

237
00:38:23.611 --> 00:38:35.273
Harness Engineering adds a second, implementation-level view of the same system: treat the LLM as one core component (the Model), and call all the supporting code built around it the Harness.

238
00:38:35.273 --> 00:38:41.336
The two views are not rivals; they describe the same system at different levels of abstraction.

239
00:38:41.336 --> 00:38:51.073
We switch to the more general word "Model" because the principles of Harness Engineering apply to any model that can reason and call tools, not one particular kind.

240
00:38:51.073 --> 00:39:06.298
The core of the Harness is the original formula's "Context + Tools," plus three layers of safeguards: Constrain (what the Agent may and may not do), Verify (whether it did the thing correctly), and Correct (how to recover when it did not).

241
00:39:06.298 --> 00:39:11.248
Expanded as an equation, the complete production-grade composition is:

242
00:39:11.248 --> 00:39:14.161
Agent = Model + Harness

243
00:39:14.161 --> 00:39:21.123
Harness = Context management + Tool interfaces + Constraints + Verification + Correction

244
00:39:21.123 --> 00:39:23.873
Agent ↔ Environment

245
00:39:23.873 --> 00:39:35.336
A minimal demo needs only a Model and a Harness that can construct context and expose tools; a production system must add constraints, verification, and correction within that same boundary.

246
00:39:35.336 --> 00:39:48.086
For example, a refund Agent can place its policy in context, constrain calls with permission and amount rules, verify the result against database state, and retry or fall back after a timeout.

247
00:39:48.086 --> 00:39:55.573
Harness engineering studies precisely this runtime and governance code—outside the Model but inside the Agent boundary.

248
00:39:55.573 --> 00:40:04.598
More precisely, the Harness is not everything outside the model: it is the runtime and governance layer inside the Agent boundary and outside the Model.

249
00:40:04.598 --> 00:40:10.198
It mediates the Model–Environment interaction but does not include the Environment itself.

250
00:40:10.198 --> 00:40:25.848
Tool definitions, call adapters, sandbox permissions and reset mechanisms belong to the Harness; files and processes that change inside the sandbox, external databases, web pages, users and the physical world belong to the Environment.

251
00:40:25.848 --> 00:40:30.036
Deployment location does not change this conceptual boundary.

252
00:40:30.036 --> 00:40:37.636
The core of the Harness is context management and tool interfaces, around which three types of engineering safeguards are built:

253
00:40:37.636 --> 00:40:44.861
Component: Context management; Responsibility and design principle: Supplies relevant information.

254
00:40:44.861 --> 00:41:00.523
Information sufficiency: Give the Agent enough information to make an informed decision at each step.; Practical examples: System prompts, knowledge bases, Agent status bars, Sidecar bypass queries; See chapters: Chapters 2 & 3.

255
00:41:00.523 --> 00:41:07.648
Component: Tool interfaces; Responsibility and design principle: Defines how the model can act.

256
00:41:07.648 --> 00:41:22.686
Clear interfaces: Use intuitive tool names, provide parameter examples, and explain each tool's limits.; Practical examples: MCP tools, code interpreter, search tools; See chapters: Chapter 4.

257
00:41:22.686 --> 00:41:29.823
Component: Constraints; Responsibility and design principle: Defines what the Agent is allowed to do.

258
00:41:29.823 --> 00:41:45.173
Fail-safe defaults: Keep capabilities disabled until explicitly enabled, as with mobile app permissions.; Practical examples: Claude Code requires user authorization before tool execution by default.; See chapters: Chapter 4.

259
00:41:45.173 --> 00:41:53.473
Component: Verification; Responsibility and design principle: Checks whether tool execution produced the correct result.

260
00:41:53.473 --> 00:42:12.236
Input isolation: Base security checks on structured data, such as fields returned by tools, rather than model-generated text that an attacker could influence through prompt injection.; Practical examples: Linting, type checks, validation of tool results; See chapters: Chapters 5 & 6.

261
00:42:12.236 --> 00:42:20.323
Component: Correction; Responsibility and design principle: Recovers from errors or rolls back failed operations.

262
00:42:20.323 --> 00:42:39.861
Attempt recovery before presenting a failure to the user—for example, retry a failed tool call instead of showing an incomplete result.; Practical examples: Automatic retries, resuming generation, escalation to a human after repeated failures (a circuit breaker); See chapters: Chapters 2 & 5.

263
00:42:40.012 --> 00:42:44.424
The basic model control loop is shown in the following pseudocode:

264
00:42:44.374 --> 00:42:49.549
In formal loop pseudocode: The environment provides an initial observation.

265
00:42:49.549 --> 00:42:56.262
In a continuous loop, the Harness builds context from the trajectory, and the Model generates actions.

266
00:42:56.262 --> 00:43:03.449
The Harness validates and constrains these actions, applies them to the environment, and observes the updated state.

267
00:43:03.449 --> 00:43:08.512
If safety checks pass, the loop continues until the goal is achieved.

268
00:43:08.512 --> 00:43:12.637
This skeleton deliberately omits implementation details.

269
00:43:12.637 --> 00:43:21.824
The complete API message loop appears in Chapter 2; tools and automatic verification are covered in Chapters 4 and 5, respectively.

270
00:43:21.824 --> 00:43:27.499
Context and Tools let the Agent complete tasks—understand the task and act on it.

271
00:43:27.499 --> 00:43:39.387
Constrain, Verify, and Correct make sure it does so reliably and safely—not as something apart from Context and Tools, but as the engineering that keeps them working reliably in production.

272
00:43:39.387 --> 00:43:45.274
Along the maturity curve of Agent products, the emphasis between these two groups shifts.

273
00:43:45.274 --> 00:43:53.637
Early Agent frameworks focused on Context and Tools: give the model tools, give it context, and let it complete tasks.

274
00:43:53.637 --> 00:44:05.174
Production-grade systems have shifted their center of gravity to Constrain, Verify, and Correct: making sure tool calls are safe, context is managed, and errors are correctable.

275
00:44:05.174 --> 00:44:07.199
Take Claude Code.

276
00:44:07.199 --> 00:44:23.687
The vast majority of its Harness code does Constrain, Verify, and Correct, not Context and Tools—the tools themselves (file read/write, command execution, search) are only a small part; the safeguards built around them are the true core.

277
00:44:23.687 --> 00:44:26.174
These mechanisms include:

278
00:44:26.174 --> 00:44:31.462
Process State Management: Tracks which step the Agent is currently executing

279
00:44:31.462 --> 00:44:37.299
Multi-Layer Context Compression: Automatically prunes information when there is too much

280
00:44:37.299 --> 00:44:42.924
Permission Classification: Controls which operations require user confirmation

281
00:44:42.924 --> 00:44:51.437
Circuit Breaker: Automatically stops retrying after repeated errors so one failing operation does not cascade through the whole system

282
00:44:51.437 --> 00:45:00.374
Error Recovery Mechanisms: Catches exceptions, rolls back to the last stable state, retries, or hands off to a human

283
00:45:00.374 --> 00:45:09.224
The industry is shifting from task completion to reliable task completion, making Harness Engineering the core competitive advantage of Agent systems.

284
00:45:09.224 --> 00:45:14.874
From Prompt Engineering to Loop Engineering: The Evolution of Engineering Paradigms.

285
00:45:14.874 --> 00:45:21.349
Looking back at the development of AI application engineering, a clear evolutionary arc emerges:

286
00:45:21.349 --> 00:45:29.574
Prompt Engineering was the first wave of innovation—improving output quality by refining the natural-language instructions fed to the model.

287
00:45:29.574 --> 00:45:45.512
Context Engineering was the second wave—the realization that optimizing the prompt alone is not enough: all the information the model can see (system instructions, tool definitions, conversation history, external knowledge) has to be managed systematically.

288
00:45:45.512 --> 00:46:02.274
Harness Engineering was the third wave—it widens the view from "what information the model receives" to "what kind of system the model runs in," taking in all infrastructure outside the model: constraint mechanisms, verification methods, feedback loops, error recovery.

289
00:46:02.428 --> 00:46:19.328
Loop Engineering came next, widening the view from a single run to sustained autonomous operation across runs: who discovers the next piece of work, when to verify, and when the task counts as truly done (Chapter 10 develops this alongside multi-agent collaboration systems).

290
00:46:19.278 --> 00:46:41.690
In July 2026, the industry began using Graph Engineering for a higher-level orchestration perspective: organizing Agent loops, deterministic programs, and human approvals into an explicit execution graph, where nodes provide capabilities, edges define routing and dependencies, and structured state travels along those edges and is persisted at key boundaries.

291
00:46:41.690 --> 00:46:53.290
These five stages are not replacements but nested layers: Prompt Engineering is a subset of Context Engineering, which is a subset of Harness Engineering, which is a subset of Loop Engineering.

292
00:46:53.290 --> 00:47:02.028
Loop Engineering is, in turn, a subset of Graph Engineering: a single Agent loop forms one node in the execution graph.

293
00:47:02.028 --> 00:47:07.378
Each layer widens the engineer's scope of concern and influence beyond the last.

294
00:47:07.378 --> 00:47:16.115
As models converge in capability and stop being the decisive differentiator, competitive advantage shifts to the engineering outside the model.

295
00:47:16.115 --> 00:47:19.565
Recent engineering practice supports this view.

296
00:47:19.565 --> 00:47:38.940
LangChain's work on Terminal Bench 2.0 (a benchmark evaluating an Agent's ability to complete complex tasks in a terminal environment) is a striking example: their Coding Agent improved from 52.8% to 66.5% (jumping from outside the top 30 to the top 5 on the leaderboard).

297
00:47:38.940 --> 00:47:49.253
What changed was not the model but the Harness—having the Agent check its own execution results, detect when it was stuck in a repetitive loop, and refine its reasoning strategy.

298
00:47:49.253 --> 00:47:52.653
Core Principles for Building Effective Agents.

299
00:47:52.653 --> 00:47:58.753
Based on Anthropic's experience, successful Agent systems follow three core principles.

300
00:47:58.753 --> 00:48:00.528
Keep it simple.

301
00:48:00.528 --> 00:48:05.803
Start with the simplest solution and add complexity only when truly necessary.

302
00:48:05.803 --> 00:48:16.753
Direct API calls are preferable to complex frameworks; clear code is preferable to clever abstraction—every extra layer of abstraction is a new blind spot during debugging.

303
00:48:16.753 --> 00:48:18.790
Keep it transparent.

304
00:48:18.790 --> 00:48:24.715
Show the Agent's planning steps, execution logs, and decision trajectory clearly.

305
00:48:24.715 --> 00:48:34.215
This is not just a debugging convenience; it is a precondition for user trust—an error inside a black box is hard to locate or fix from outside.

306
00:48:34.215 --> 00:48:39.728
Design a well-structured tool interface (ACI, Agent-Computer Interface).

307
00:48:39.728 --> 00:48:50.278
ACI means designing the interface from the Agent's perspective—easy for the Agent to understand and use—rather than from the programmer's perspective, as in traditional APIs.

308
00:48:50.278 --> 00:49:05.728
Tool names and parameters should be intuitive, and wherever misuse is likely the design should make the mistake impossible from the start: a SIM card's notched corner lets it slide into the tray in only one orientation, and a microwave refuses to heat while its door is open.

309
00:49:05.728 --> 00:49:15.678
In manufacturing, this approach is known as poka-yoke, or mistake-proofing: designing products and processes to prevent mistakes or make them immediately apparent.

310
00:49:15.678 --> 00:49:18.828
It is used in the Toyota Production System.

311
00:49:18.828 --> 00:49:30.303
A poorly designed tool can cause even the strongest model to fail repeatedly: the interface is the only channel between model and tool, and a vague interface gets amplified into systemic error.

312
00:49:30.303 --> 00:49:40.178
The next three sections address three freestanding but important topics in Harness engineering: model selection, orchestration patterns, and guardrails and safety.

313
00:49:40.178 --> 00:49:46.515
None belongs to the five Harness elements proper, but all are unavoidable in engineering practice.

314
00:49:46.515 --> 00:49:48.603
How to Choose a Model.

315
00:49:48.603 --> 00:49:56.215
Before discussing orchestration patterns, we first need to answer a practical question: what kind of model should drive your Agent?

316
00:49:56.215 --> 00:50:03.915
The model is the foundation of the Agent's intelligence, and choosing the right one often matters more than any amount of prompt tuning.

317
00:50:03.915 --> 00:50:11.865
Model releases move too quickly for specific version recommendations to stay useful, so this section offers directions instead.

318
00:50:12.028 --> 00:50:14.253
Closed-Source Models.

319
00:50:14.203 --> 00:50:24.903
The two most commonly used closed-source model providers in current Agent development are OpenAI (GPT/o series) and Anthropic (Claude series).

320
00:50:24.903 --> 00:50:32.490
Closed-source models generally lead in capability but are more expensive and constrained by the vendor's API policies.

321
00:50:32.490 --> 00:50:40.165
When selecting a model, do not rely only on leaderboards; evaluate it on your own tasks (see Chapter 7).

322
00:50:40.165 --> 00:50:42.303
Open-Source Models.

323
00:50:42.303 --> 00:50:50.678
At the time of writing, open-source models lag closed-source models by no more than six months, while costing substantially less.

324
00:50:50.678 --> 00:50:57.953
If your business scenario does not demand the highest model capability, an open-source model is a pragmatic choice.

325
00:50:57.953 --> 00:51:09.640
Open-source models are low-cost, support private deployment, and allow fine-tuning customization, making them suitable for cost-sensitive scenarios or those with data compliance requirements.

326
00:51:09.640 --> 00:51:16.203
DeepSeek, Kimi, and GLM are among the stronger Chinese models for Agent capabilities.

327
00:51:16.203 --> 00:51:23.478
Note that models differ widely in tool-calling ability, so be sure to test in your specific scenario before committing.

328
00:51:23.478 --> 00:51:27.953
Beyond capability, consider the model's policy boundaries.

329
00:51:27.953 --> 00:51:35.303
A model may have the technical ability to perform a task without the product that hosts it allowing users to invoke that ability.

330
00:51:35.303 --> 00:51:50.753
Vendors draw different policy boundaries around cybersecurity, model distillation, model extraction, private data, and high-risk operations; the same task may also produce different outcomes in a chat product, a Coding Agent, and an API.

331
00:51:50.753 --> 00:51:56.415
Model selection therefore cannot compare only accuracy, price, and speed.

332
00:51:56.415 --> 00:52:06.453
Test on your real tasks whether the model is willing to proceed, whether the interface exposes the required capability, and whether the service terms permit the intended use.

333
00:52:06.453 --> 00:52:12.765
For business-critical tasks, prepare human handoff or another compliant model as a fallback.

334
00:52:12.765 --> 00:52:16.140
Most Agents Need a Model that Supports Reasoning.

335
00:52:16.140 --> 00:52:24.728
Agents make complex decisions—multi-step reasoning, tool selection—and models without reasoning tend to perform poorly on them.

336
00:52:24.728 --> 00:52:34.990
The exceptions are few: a single simple step, or Computer Use GUI operations that amount to clicking a fixed position, where a non-reasoning model may suffice.

337
00:52:34.990 --> 00:52:41.290
The moment multi-step reasoning or dynamic decision-making enters, a reasoning model is essential.

338
00:52:41.290 --> 00:52:45.315
Consider Output Speed and Multimodal Capabilities.

339
00:52:45.315 --> 00:52:49.390
Beyond cost, two dimensions are easy to overlook.

340
00:52:49.390 --> 00:53:06.278
One is output token speed: Agents typically run many rounds of inference, and each round must finish before the next can start, so output speed directly determines end-to-end latency—a 20-round Agent task that runs 2 seconds slower per round means an extra 40 seconds of waiting.

341
00:53:06.278 --> 00:53:17.603
The other is multimodal support: if your Agent needs to understand images, audio, or video, multimodal capability is a hard requirement, and models differ widely here.

342
00:53:17.603 --> 00:53:21.815
Orchestration Patterns: Workflow vs. Autonomous.

343
00:53:21.815 --> 00:53:37.215
Orchestration patterns are how the Harness organizes its "context and tools" layer—they determine how context flows between LLM calls, how tools are scheduled, and whether the Agent's execution path is fixed in advance or generated dynamically.

344
00:53:37.215 --> 00:53:44.278
Agent orchestration has evolved from simple to complex, and each pattern has suitable use cases and trade-offs.

345
00:53:44.278 --> 00:53:55.740
In Anthropic's experience working with dozens of teams building LLM Agents, the most successful implementations rarely use complex frameworks; they use simple, composable patterns.

346
00:53:55.740 --> 00:54:01.953
When building an LLM application, follow the principle of progressing from simple to complex.

347
00:54:01.953 --> 00:54:05.503
Start by considering a single LLM call.

348
00:54:05.503 --> 00:54:11.853
If better prompts and in-context examples can solve the problem, do not introduce an Agent system.

349
00:54:11.853 --> 00:54:19.578
When multi-step processing is needed, consider a workflow for scenarios that decompose cleanly into fixed subtasks.

350
00:54:19.578 --> 00:54:25.853
Use an autonomous Agent only when dynamic decisions and flexible execution paths are required.

351
00:54:25.853 --> 00:54:34.103
Remember that Agent systems typically trade latency and cost for better task performance, so carefully weigh whether that trade is worthwhile.

352
00:54:34.252 --> 00:54:40.577
A common counterexample is to begin by building a highly complex workflow or multi-Agent system.

353
00:54:40.527 --> 00:55:05.239
For example, when asked to design an Agent that “extracts personal memories from a million chat messages,” some AI models will quickly sketch a seemingly rigorous pipeline: first segment the conversations, then assign extraction, evidence verification, identity resolution, memory curation, and merge-review Agents in sequence, and finally build a fact graph, a coverage ledger, and immutable versions.

354
00:55:05.239 --> 00:55:12.114
Each component makes sense in isolation, yet together they form a highly inefficient and unreliable system.

355
00:55:12.114 --> 00:55:23.189
Because a complex workflow has a fixed execution topology, each new exception readily leads to another node: the architecture grows increasingly complex while becoming less general.

356
00:55:23.189 --> 00:55:29.214
Semantic judgments that the model could have made from context are instead hard-coded into the workflow.

357
00:55:29.214 --> 00:55:34.702
Therefore, the importance of the Harness does not mean that a more complex Harness is better.

358
00:55:34.702 --> 00:55:40.402
The Manus website summarizes this trade-off as “Less structure, more intelligence.

359
00:55:40.402 --> 00:55:53.752
Start by giving a capable Agent a clear goal, the necessary context, and composable tools, and use natural language to tell it how a person would verify evidence, make comparisons, and resolve conflicts.

360
00:55:53.752 --> 00:56:02.827
The program should hard-code only boundaries that must always hold, such as permissions, never overwriting source material, and atomic publication.

361
00:56:02.827 --> 00:56:16.864
Only when business constraints inherently require it, or evaluations repeatedly expose a stable failure mode, should the corresponding step be promoted into a dedicated verifier, an independent Agent, or a deterministic process.

362
00:56:16.864 --> 00:56:24.889
Good structure does not rehearse all of the Agent's thinking for it; it guards the boundaries and returns the decision space within them to the model.

363
00:56:24.889 --> 00:56:28.564
Workflow Pattern: Deterministic Orchestration.

364
00:56:28.564 --> 00:56:34.639
A workflow is a system that orchestrates LLMs and tools through predefined code paths.

365
00:56:34.639 --> 00:56:47.239
Its execution path is deterministic and designed in advance by the developer—the behavior of each step and transition is defined in code; the LLM handles only the understanding and generation inside each node.

366
00:56:47.239 --> 00:56:52.689
For example, a flight-booking Agent can use a workflow with four fixed nodes:

367
00:56:52.689 --> 00:56:59.277
Verify User Identity—Call the identity verification API to confirm who the user is.

368
00:56:59.277 --> 00:57:05.039
Search for Available Flights—Query the flight database based on user requirements.

369
00:57:05.039 --> 00:57:09.539
Complete Payment—Call the payment interface to deduct the amount.

370
00:57:09.539 --> 00:57:15.652
Confirm Booking—Call the booking API to lock the seat and send a confirmation to the user.

371
00:57:15.652 --> 00:57:32.752
An LLM can be used within each node (e.g., using natural language to understand the user's travel needs), but the flow sequence between nodes is fixed by code—the system will not book a seat before payment is completed, nor will it start searching for flights before identity verification.

372
00:57:32.752 --> 00:57:36.214
The workflow pattern has two core advantages.

373
00:57:36.214 --> 00:57:50.064
First, strict process control: the developer can guarantee that critical steps are never skipped or run out of order—business rules like "no booking before payment" are enforced by code, not left to the LLM's judgment.

374
00:57:50.064 --> 00:58:03.239
Second, security: because the execution path is deterministic, prompt injection or a model error can at most affect the processing inside the current node; it cannot make the Agent jump to a branch it should not reach.

375
00:58:03.239 --> 00:58:06.802
The attack surface is confined to a single node.

376
00:58:06.802 --> 00:58:11.052
The main limitation of a workflow is its lack of flexibility.

377
00:58:11.052 --> 00:58:28.014
When an unanticipated event occurs—for example, the user changes the booking during payment, or a flight is canceled and the system needs to recommend an alternative—the fixed path cannot adapt on its own; it can only follow a preset exception branch or hand control back to a human.

378
00:58:28.180 --> 00:58:32.767
Take the simplest workflow example: text-to-image generation.

379
00:58:32.717 --> 00:58:50.530
The user's need is usually a single plain-language sentence, such as "Draw me a scene of programmers at work after AGI is achieved"; but text-to-image models like Stable Diffusion only accept prompts in a specific style—comma-separated English tags, quality words, negative prompts.

380
00:58:50.530 --> 00:58:56.242
So the workflow places two fixed nodes between the user and the image-generation model:

381
00:58:56.242 --> 00:59:04.592
Prompt Rewriting—Use an LLM to rewrite the user's natural-language request into the prompt format a text-to-image model expects.

382
00:59:04.592 --> 00:59:27.967
For the example above, "programmers at work after AGI is achieved" is a very broad requirement, so the LLM also needs to think carefully (for example, "after AGI is achieved, programmers no longer need to write code, so the image should show a programmer sunbathing on a beach, directing AI employees through a brain-computer interface") and then produce a concrete scene description.

383
00:59:27.967 --> 00:59:33.830
Image Generation—Call the text-to-image model with the rewritten prompt to obtain the image.

384
00:59:33.830 --> 00:59:36.830
The execution path is hard-coded.

385
00:59:36.830 --> 00:59:49.367
The LLM node in this workflow performs translation—converting human language into an input format the tool can understand—and it exists because text-to-image models "don't understand plain speech.

386
00:59:49.367 --> 00:59:57.292
Harness code that specifically patches a capability shortcoming of a tool (or model) like this can be called an adapter layer.

387
00:59:57.292 --> 01:00:09.280
But if the image-generation tool is replaced with a multimodal model that has native image generation capability, such as Nano Banana 2 or GPT-Image 2, prompt rewriting is no longer needed.

388
01:00:09.280 --> 01:00:16.267
No matter how the user phrases the request, the model understands it on its own and produces the image directly.

389
01:00:16.267 --> 01:00:24.155
Experiment 1-4 introductory difficulty, one star: : Text-to-Image Workflow vs. Native Image Generation

390
01:00:24.155 --> 01:00:27.967
Send the same plain-language request through two routes.

391
01:00:27.967 --> 01:00:47.242
Workflow route: an LLM first rewrites the request into a Stable Diffusion-style prompt, then calls the text-to-image model to produce the image; native route: send the sentence as-is to a multimodal model that supports native image generation (such as GPT-Image 2), producing the image in a single call.

392
01:00:47.242 --> 01:00:56.217
Compare two things: what the prompt-rewriting node turns the original request into, and which route's image stays closer to the original request.

393
01:00:56.217 --> 01:01:11.992
It is worth comparing two categories of requests: one that is concrete (for example, a poster with specified copy), and one that is broad (such as the AGI work scene above)—for this category, the workflow route may still have advantages of its own.

394
01:01:12.148 --> 01:01:21.373
This experiment shows that the parts of a Harness that patch the model's capability shortcomings will be internalized by the model itself as the model grows stronger.

395
01:01:21.323 --> 01:01:46.573
In this first chapter alone, this has already happened several times: few-shot examples and prompting tricks like "let's think step by step" were internalized by instruction tuning and reasoning models; output-format repair and JSON parsing tolerance were internalized by structured output and native tool calling; and text-to-image prompt rewriting was absorbed by the model's native multimodal understanding and generation capabilities.

396
01:01:46.573 --> 01:01:53.423
Each round of internalization eliminates adapter-layer code of the "translation" and "scaffolding" kind.

397
01:01:53.423 --> 01:01:56.998
Autonomous Agent: Runtime Decision-Making.

398
01:01:56.998 --> 01:02:02.223
When the fixed path of a workflow is insufficient, we need an autonomous Agent.

399
01:02:02.223 --> 01:02:12.435
The core difference between an autonomous Agent and a workflow is that the execution path is not predefined but is determined at runtime by the Agent based on environmental feedback.

400
01:02:12.435 --> 01:02:18.160
Returning to the flight example, an autonomous Agent needs no four predefined nodes.

401
01:02:18.160 --> 01:02:31.498
The user says, "Book me a flight to Shanghai next Wednesday," and the Agent determines the sequence dynamically: it searches for flights, discovers that login is required, verifies identity, and resumes the search.

402
01:02:31.498 --> 01:02:39.735
If the cheapest flight has a layover, it can ask whether that is acceptable; if the user says no, it adjusts the search criteria.

403
01:02:39.735 --> 01:02:49.973
An autonomous Agent therefore has to plan for itself—choose its own execution steps—and recognize failure and change strategy rather than simply halting on error.

404
01:02:49.973 --> 01:03:04.860
But autonomy is not unbounded: explicit stopping conditions must be designed in (task complete, maximum iterations reached, unrecoverable error hit), or the Agent can enter infinite loops or continue executing after the task is already done.

405
01:03:04.860 --> 01:03:17.885
From an implementation perspective, an autonomous Agent is essentially an LLM using tools in a loop, continuously obtaining environmental feedback to make progress on the task—this is the ReAct loop introduced earlier.

406
01:03:17.885 --> 01:03:29.148
Common exit conditions include: calling a final output tool, the model returning a response without any tool calls, or encountering an error or reaching the maximum number of rounds.

407
01:03:29.148 --> 01:03:34.835
As illustrated in Figure 1-6: Execution loop of an autonomous Agent.

408
01:03:34.835 --> 01:03:42.060
Autonomous Agents are well suited to open-ended problems—those where it is difficult to predict the number of steps required.

409
01:03:42.060 --> 01:04:02.448
Typical use cases include: Coding Agents solving SWE-bench (Software Engineering Benchmark, a benchmark for evaluating an Agent's ability to automatically fix real GitHub issues) tasks, "Computer Use" Agents operating computer interfaces like a human, and research tasks requiring iterative search and analysis.

410
01:04:02.448 --> 01:04:06.548
Autonomy also costs more and lets errors compound.

411
01:04:06.548 --> 01:04:17.210
Deploying an autonomous Agent therefore demands thorough testing in a sandbox, appropriate guardrails and monitoring, and human-in-the-loop checkpoints at critical decision points.

412
01:04:17.210 --> 01:04:20.098
Choosing and Mixing the Two Patterns.

413
01:04:20.098 --> 01:04:35.923
In practice, workflows and autonomous Agents are not mutually exclusive—many systems mix the two: critical processes with strict compliance requirements run as workflows for reliability, while the parts that need flexible decisions switch to autonomous mode.

414
01:04:35.923 --> 01:04:50.835
n8n, for example, is a mature open-source workflow automation framework in which developers build Agents by arranging functional components on a visual canvas—and workflow nodes and autonomous Agent nodes can coexist in the same system.

415
01:04:50.835 --> 01:04:56.460
As illustrated in Figure 1-7: n8n workflow editor interface.

416
01:04:56.620 --> 01:05:03.807
Another way to mix them is to have the autonomous Agent write the workflow first, and then let the workflow do the executing.

417
01:05:03.757 --> 01:05:15.795
After reading the task, the Agent decides the topology itself and generates a piece of orchestration code; once that code exists, the execution phase falls back to the determinism of a workflow.

418
01:05:15.795 --> 01:05:24.070
This keeps the autonomous Agent's flexibility in the face of unfamiliar tasks while sparing the model from making a decision at every dispatch.

419
01:05:24.070 --> 01:05:27.445
Chapter 10 discusses this form in detail.

420
01:05:27.445 --> 01:05:31.182
Brief Comparison of Mainstream Agent Frameworks.

421
01:05:31.182 --> 01:05:38.970
The following table summarizes widely used Agent frameworks and platforms to help readers identify the right one for their scenario:

422
01:05:38.970 --> 01:05:57.395
Framework/Platform: Codex Harness; Core Positioning: Open-source Agent runtime behind Codex; Orchestration Pattern: Autonomous; Development Approach: Code-first, embeddable in your own app; Suitable Scenarios: Coding Agents, embedding an Agent into your own product.

423
01:05:57.395 --> 01:06:14.332
Framework/Platform: Claude Agent SDK; Core Positioning: Production-grade Agent development framework; Orchestration Pattern: Autonomous; Development Approach: Code-first; Suitable Scenarios: Complex autonomous tasks, Coding Agents.

424
01:06:14.332 --> 01:06:32.520
Framework/Platform: LangChain / LangGraph; Core Positioning: General LLM application framework; Orchestration Pattern: Workflow + autonomous; Development Approach: Code-first; Suitable Scenarios: Complex reasoning chains, multi-step workflows.

425
01:06:32.520 --> 01:06:48.482
Framework/Platform: n8n; Core Positioning: Visual workflow automation; Orchestration Pattern: Workflow + autonomous; Development Approach: Low-code; Suitable Scenarios: Business automation, nontechnical teams.

426
01:06:48.482 --> 01:07:05.920
Framework/Platform: Dify; Core Positioning: LLM application development platform; Orchestration Pattern: Workflow + conversational; Development Approach: Low-code + API; Suitable Scenarios: Enterprise RAG, knowledge-base applications.

427
01:07:05.920 --> 01:07:22.357
Framework/Platform: CrewAI; Core Positioning: Role-based multi-Agent orchestration; Orchestration Pattern: Multi-Agent collaboration; Development Approach: Code-first; Suitable Scenarios: Team-style task decomposition and execution.

428
01:07:22.357 --> 01:07:42.032
Framework/Platform: OpenClaw; Core Positioning: Open-source all-purpose personal Agent; Orchestration Pattern: Autonomous + event-driven; Development Approach: Configuration + code; Suitable Scenarios: Personal assistants, Deep Research, Computer Use, multiplatform messaging.

429
01:07:42.032 --> 01:07:58.632
Framework/Platform: DeepSeek Harness; Core Positioning: Agent self-evolution framework; Orchestration Pattern: Everything is a plugin; Development Approach: Code-first, easy to customize; Suitable Scenarios: Agent developers, researchers.

430
01:07:58.632 --> 01:08:13.345
Framework/Platform: Pi; Core Positioning: Minimal Coding Agent framework; Orchestration Pattern: Autonomous; Development Approach: Code-first, easy to customize; Suitable Scenarios: Agent developers.

431
01:08:13.345 --> 01:08:17.095
The first two rows deserve a separate clarification.

432
01:08:17.095 --> 01:08:27.532
Codex is OpenAI's Coding Agent product (App, CLI, IDE extension), and the Codex Harness is the runtime layer that drives all of these forms.

433
01:08:27.532 --> 01:08:51.320
The Codex Harness offers three integration paths: codex exec suits one-off tasks in scripts and CI; the Codex SDK suits third-party application code that starts, resumes, and streams tasks; and the app-server provides persistent sessions, event streams, and approval callbacks over the JSON-RPC protocol, which suits building an Agent directly into a product.

434
01:08:51.320 --> 01:09:02.770
Claude Agent SDK and Claude Code stand in a similar relationship, except that what Claude opens up is the SDK interface—the Harness implementation itself is not open source.

435
01:09:02.770 --> 01:09:05.645
Agent frameworks evolve rapidly.

436
01:09:05.645 --> 01:09:12.307
By the time you read this book, some of these frameworks may already be obsolete and new ones may be popular.

437
01:09:12.307 --> 01:09:17.282
Learning the API of one particular framework is therefore not important.

438
01:09:17.282 --> 01:09:25.945
When choosing a framework, the key question is not its sophistication, but whether its abstraction is thin enough to let you focus on business logic.

439
01:09:26.116 --> 01:09:35.403
Orchestration patterns solve the organization of context and tools within the Harness—how LLM calls, tools, and data flows connect.

440
01:09:35.353 --> 01:09:41.366
But task completion is not enough; tasks must also be completed correctly and safely.

441
01:09:41.366 --> 01:09:48.053
We therefore turn to guardrails, which put constraints, verification, and correction into practice.

442
01:09:48.053 --> 01:09:50.341
Guardrails and Safety.

443
01:09:50.341 --> 01:10:00.266
Guardrails implement the Harness’s constraints, verification, and correction mechanisms, providing a layered defense that keeps Agent behavior safe and controllable.

444
01:10:00.266 --> 01:10:12.378
Well-designed guardrails help manage data privacy risks (for example, preventing system prompt leakage) and reputational risks (for example, keeping model behavior consistent with the brand).

445
01:10:12.378 --> 01:10:19.403
Start with guardrails for the risks you have already identified, then add new ones as new vulnerabilities surface.

446
01:10:19.403 --> 01:10:22.516
Think of guardrails as defense in depth.

447
01:10:22.516 --> 01:10:31.003
No single guardrail is likely to be sufficient on its own, but several specialized ones combined make a far more resilient Agent system.

448
01:10:31.003 --> 01:10:35.366
Guardrails also have another failure mode: false refusal.

449
01:10:35.366 --> 01:10:46.428
To reduce the chance of allowing dangerous requests, a model may also reject legitimate but sensitive-looking work, such as authorized security testing or model distillation research.

450
01:10:46.428 --> 01:10:55.666
Guardrail evaluation should therefore test not only whether prohibited requests are blocked, but also whether clearly permitted requests can still be completed.

451
01:10:55.666 --> 01:10:57.803
Types of Guardrails.

452
01:10:57.803 --> 01:11:04.766
Guardrails can be placed at three layers: the context layer, the execution layer, and the data layer.

453
01:11:04.766 --> 01:11:18.216
These three are ordered not by where they sit in the request lifecycle, but by how hard they are to bypass—the lower the layer, the less it depends on the model's own judgment, and the harder it is for a single successful attack to get through.

454
01:11:18.216 --> 01:11:23.578
The book uses this three-layer framework to organize its later security discussions.

455
01:11:23.578 --> 01:11:30.578
Context-layer guardrails govern what the model gets to see, intercepting content before it enters the context.

456
01:11:30.578 --> 01:11:33.778
They usually comprise four mechanisms.

457
01:11:33.778 --> 01:11:41.303
A relevance classifier flags off-topic queries—a coding assistant asked "how tall is the Empire State Building?

458
01:11:41.303 --> 01:12:02.328
A safety classifier detects jailbreaks (inducing the model to bypass its safety limits) and prompt injection (embedding malicious instructions in the input); the key difference is that a jailbreak is the user trying to get around the model's own limits, whereas prompt injection is an attacker manipulating the model indirectly through external data such as web pages or documents.

459
01:12:02.328 --> 01:12:09.003
Content moderation flags harmful or inappropriate input such as violent or discriminatory content.

460
01:12:09.003 --> 01:12:19.528
Rule-based protection applies deterministic measures—blocklists, input length limits, regular-expression filters—against known threats such as SQL injection.

461
01:12:19.528 --> 01:12:27.441
Source labelling and the separation of "instructions" from "data" also belong to this layer; Chapter 2 develops them.

462
01:12:27.441 --> 01:12:34.353
Anthropic's Constitutional Classifiers provide one example of classifier-based guardrails in practice.

463
01:12:34.353 --> 01:12:37.366
Their design has three key elements.

464
01:12:37.366 --> 01:12:47.703
First, rule-driven training: natural-language rules specifying what is allowed and prohibited are used to generate synthetic training data for the input and output classifiers.

465
01:12:47.703 --> 01:13:05.641
Second, joint contextual judgment: the new generation checks the user's question and the model's answer together, because some answers look perfectly fine on their own (e.g., "how to use food flavorings"), and only against the question does it become clear that "food flavorings" is code for chemical reagents.

466
01:13:05.641 --> 01:13:22.266
Third, two-stage screening: an extremely lightweight probe—which reads the model's internal activations at almost zero cost—checks every conversation first, and anything suspicious is escalated to a more powerful classifier for review rather than being refused outright.

467
01:13:22.266 --> 01:13:30.828
This way the first stage can tolerate more false positives without hurting the user experience, and the overall cost is greatly reduced.

468
01:13:30.988 --> 01:13:39.563
But this layer has a structural ceiling: an Agent cannot reliably determine whether prompt injection has already compromised its own context.

469
01:13:39.513 --> 01:13:48.963
The context layer can therefore lower the success rate of an attack but cannot offer a guarantee—which is exactly why the two layers below it are necessary.

470
01:13:48.963 --> 01:13:55.588
Execution-layer guardrails govern what the model gets to do, validating an action before it takes effect.

471
01:13:55.588 --> 01:14:09.700
At their core is tool risk rating: each tool is labelled low, medium, or high risk according to reversibility, privilege level, and financial impact, and high-risk operations require additional review or human confirmation.

472
01:14:09.700 --> 01:14:25.538
What matters is that this review must be performed by a mechanism outside the context—an independent review process, least-privilege credentials, sandbox isolation, a human in the loop—otherwise the same attack may compromise both the Agent and its safeguards.

473
01:14:25.538 --> 01:14:47.613
The reply returned to the user is itself an action (Chapter 4 classifies it as a user-communication tool), so output checks belong to this layer too: a PII filter screens the output for personally identifiable information such as ID or phone numbers to prevent unnecessary exposure, and output validation checks content to keep replies aligned with brand values.

474
01:14:47.613 --> 01:14:52.763
Data-layer guardrails govern which changes the system is allowed to make to its data.

475
01:14:52.763 --> 01:15:10.013
They enforce who may perform which operations on which records through stable, human-reviewed mechanisms: row-level security policies in the database, constraints and validators, controlled views and stored procedures, and an access context bound by a trusted runtime that cannot be forged.

476
01:15:10.013 --> 01:15:24.175
The value of this layer is precisely that it does not depend on the two above it being correct—even if the prompt injection succeeds and the generated code omits its permission checks entirely, the unauthorized operation is still rejected at the data layer.

477
01:15:24.175 --> 01:15:29.800
Chapter 5 develops this layer through the example of dynamically generated software.

478
01:15:29.800 --> 01:15:31.800
Human Intervention.

479
01:15:31.800 --> 01:15:40.325
Human-in-the-loop intervention is a key protective measure: it lets an Agent improve real-world performance without degrading the user experience.

480
01:15:40.325 --> 01:15:49.425
It matters most in early deployment, when it helps identify failure modes, surface edge cases, and establish a robust evaluation cycle.

481
01:15:49.425 --> 01:15:55.963
With a human-in-the-loop mechanism, an Agent that cannot complete a task can hand over control gracefully.

482
01:15:55.963 --> 01:16:04.775
In customer service, this means escalating to a human representative; for a Coding Agent, it means handing control back to the developer.

483
01:16:04.775 --> 01:16:09.350
There are typically two main situations that trigger human intervention:

484
01:16:09.350 --> 01:16:12.125
Exceeding Failure Thresholds

485
01:16:12.125 --> 01:16:16.063
Set caps on the Agent's retries and operations.

486
01:16:16.063 --> 01:16:20.213
If the Agent exceeds those caps, escalate to a human.

487
01:16:20.213 --> 01:16:22.513
High-Risk Operations

488
01:16:22.513 --> 01:16:32.225
Sensitive, irreversible, or high-risk operations should trigger human oversight—at least until the team has built enough confidence in the Agent's reliability.

489
01:16:32.225 --> 01:16:37.563
Typical examples include authorizing a large refund or processing a payment.

490
01:16:37.563 --> 01:16:43.738
Back to the main thread of the five Harness elements—let us see how they relate to the structure of this book.

491
01:16:43.738 --> 01:16:47.288
The Five Harness Elements and the "Building" Part.

492
01:16:47.288 --> 01:16:52.163
The two formulas describe the same architecture at different levels of detail.

493
01:16:52.163 --> 01:17:06.375
Agent = LLM + Context + Tools provides the organizing framework for the book: Chapters 2–6 cover construction, Chapters 7–9 cover evaluation and improvement, and Chapter 10 covers collaboration.

494
01:17:06.375 --> 01:17:18.700
Agent = Model + Harness expands context and tools into five responsibilities for a production system: context management, tool interfaces, constraints, verification, and correction.

495
01:17:18.700 --> 01:17:26.588
It therefore explains the architecture covered in the construction chapters, rather than providing a separate outline for all ten chapters.

496
01:17:26.588 --> 01:17:32.713
Within that scope, the five Harness elements map cleanly onto chapters 2 through 5:

497
01:17:32.884 --> 01:17:50.521
Harness Focus: Context Management; Corresponding Chapter: Chapter 2 (Context Engineering); Core Content: Prompt engineering, Agent status bar, context compression, Agent Skills; Security Concerns: Prompt injection and context contamination.

498
01:17:50.471 --> 01:18:09.609
Harness Focus: Context Management Across Sessions; Corresponding Chapter: Chapter 3 (User Memory and Knowledge Bases); Core Content: User memory, RAG, structured indexing, agentic RAG; Security Concerns: Sensitive information exposure, privacy protection.

499
01:18:09.609 --> 01:18:29.846
Harness Focus: Tool Interfaces and Constraints; Corresponding Chapter: Chapter 4 (Tools); Core Content: Tool classification, permission control, MCP standard, proactive tool discovery; Security Concerns: Misoperation, unauthorized access, irreversible operations.

500
01:18:29.846 --> 01:18:48.684
Harness Focus: Verification and Correction; Corresponding Chapter: Chapter 5 (Coding Agents and General-Purpose Agents); Core Content: Coding Agent's Harness, test-driven development, codified rules; Security Concerns: Identity impersonation, responsibility attribution.

501
01:18:48.684 --> 01:18:58.934
Chapter 6 (Interaction) does not belong to any of the five elements; what it expands is the modality and timing of the observation and action spaces themselves.

502
01:18:58.934 --> 01:19:04.996
Chapters 7 through 9 ask how we know the Harness was built right, and how to keep making it better.

503
01:19:04.996 --> 01:19:10.809
Chapter 10 replaces a single Agent's Harness with a collaboration structure among several.

504
01:19:10.809 --> 01:19:16.134
Forcing these chapters into the five categories would make the categories less useful.

505
01:19:16.134 --> 01:19:31.246
Security likewise is not partitioned by chapter: it is a cross-cutting concern (a problem that affects many parts of a system) running through the whole book, organized by the three guardrail layers of the previous section—context, execution, data.

506
01:19:31.246 --> 01:19:37.921
The "Security Concerns" column above identifies the main security concerns addressed in each chapter.

507
01:19:37.921 --> 01:19:45.346
Anthropic's practice in building long-running Agents shows how Harness design can solve problems the model itself cannot.

508
01:19:45.346 --> 01:20:06.746
They split complex tasks between an "Initialization Agent" (setting up the environment, decomposing the task list) and an "Execution Agent" (making incremental progress each session and leaving clear handover artifacts), using a structured Harness to tackle the two failure modes of long tasks: running out of context and declaring the task done prematurely.

509
01:20:06.746 --> 01:20:19.359
The chapters ahead work through the Harness component by component—Chapter 2 begins with the most central one, context engineering, and Chapter 5 lays out the complete practice of Harness engineering in Coding Agents.

510
01:20:19.359 --> 01:20:22.271
Design Patterns That Run Through the Book.

511
01:20:22.271 --> 01:20:29.796
The chapters that follow repeatedly use the same group of design patterns, so they are named and defined canonically here once.

512
01:20:29.796 --> 01:20:34.796
Proposer-Reviewer: the creator and reviewer work in separate contexts.

513
01:20:34.796 --> 01:20:44.184
The reviewer assesses the artifact itself—the rendered result, the test output, or the structured call arguments—rather than the creator's reasoning.

514
01:20:44.184 --> 01:20:54.484
The premise is that self-review is unreliable: a model may struggle to recognize its own blind spots or detect whether its context has been compromised by prompt injection.

515
01:20:54.484 --> 01:21:21.109
Chapter 3 uses it to update knowledge; Chapter 4 uses it for pre-approval and post-validation of tool calls (the Sidecar is a read-only variant); the PPT, video and log experiments of Chapter 5 are all built on it; Chapter 7 uses it to evaluate UIs; Chapter 9 uses it to review update proposals; and Chapter 10 discusses its shape in peer collaboration, and why an Agent must not review itself.

516
01:21:21.109 --> 01:21:30.046
Progressive Disclosure: rather than putting everything into the context at once, offer a searchable catalogue first and load the details on demand.

517
01:21:30.046 --> 01:21:36.134
It optimizes two things simultaneously—the context budget and selection accuracy.

518
01:21:36.134 --> 01:21:51.871
Agent Skills in Chapter 2 is the archetype (metadata resident, body loaded on demand); the layered retrieval of Chapter 3, the proactive tool discovery and paginated truncation of Chapter 4, and Agent discovery in Chapter 10 are all variants.

519
01:21:52.036 --> 01:21:58.361
Append-only: state evolves by appending, and what has been written is never revised in place.

520
01:21:58.311 --> 01:22:03.273
What this buys is cacheability, replayability and auditability.

521
01:22:03.273 --> 01:22:11.961
Chapter 2 shows the performance benefit of keeping the KV-cache prefix stable: changes earlier in the prefix invalidate more of the cache.

522
01:22:11.961 --> 01:22:23.086
Chapter 3's event-based memory and Chapter 4's practice of appending newly discovered tool schemas to the trajectory, rather than inserting them into the prefix, follow the same principle.

523
01:22:23.086 --> 01:22:32.911
Boundary Set + Retention Set: every change must be validated both on "the samples it is supposed to change" and on "the samples it must not affect".

524
01:22:32.911 --> 01:22:40.736
Testing only the former mistakes overfitting for progress; testing only the latter mistakes an ineffective change for a safe one.

525
01:22:40.736 --> 01:22:51.661
The regression tasks of Chapter 7, the training/evaluation isolation of Chapter 8, and the update-proposal validation of Chapter 9 all rest on this pair of sets.

526
01:22:51.661 --> 01:23:01.398
Minimal Diff, Reversible: keep each change as small as possible, carrying its provenance, and independently revertible instead of rewritten wholesale.

527
01:23:01.398 --> 01:23:07.998
This is what makes attribution possible—when something breaks, it can be traced to one specific change.

528
01:23:07.998 --> 01:23:26.661
The knowledge updates of Chapter 3, the code patches of Chapter 5, and the prompt and program updates of Chapter 9 all follow it; and the three update paths given at the start of this chapter (in-context adaptation, external-artifact updates, parameter updates) are themselves ordered from most to least reversible.

529
01:23:26.661 --> 01:23:28.548
Chapter Summary.

530
01:23:28.548 --> 01:23:34.511
This chapter has built a practice-first framework for understanding and constructing AI Agents.

531
01:23:34.511 --> 01:23:49.098
Agent = Reasoning Engine + Working Context + Action Interfaces: The LLM provides reasoning and decision-making, context supplies the working set of information available at decision time, and tools provide the action interfaces.

532
01:23:49.098 --> 01:23:51.798
None of the three is dispensable.

533
01:23:51.798 --> 01:24:08.611
Expanding Context and Tools Is the Primary Capability Lever: Once the model is fixed, redefining or enlarging the observation and action spaces—that is, expanding context and tools—can often turn an unsolvable task into a solvable one directly.

534
01:24:08.611 --> 01:24:20.986
The evolution of both Manus and OpenClaw shows that much of an Agent's versatility comes from expanding the interfaces available to it; that expansion must remain on-demand and be paired with permissions and verification.

535
01:24:20.986 --> 01:24:31.448
Context Is the Decisive Factor: Context consists of a static prefix (system prompt + tool definitions) and a dynamic trajectory (message history).

536
01:24:31.448 --> 01:24:46.486
Ablation shows the components are not equivalent: removing tool definitions or tool results takes away the ability to act or to close the loop outright, while the cost of removing the other two depends on whether that information can be reconstructed from the current observations.

537
01:24:46.486 --> 01:24:53.573
The essence of the ReAct loop is appending to the trajectory, over and over, so the model keeps advancing the task.

538
01:24:53.573 --> 01:25:08.361
Harness Is the Competitive Advantage: Model capability is commoditizing; the real differentiator is the Harness—the constraints, verification, and correction mechanisms built around context and tools that enable reliable task completion.

539
01:25:08.361 --> 01:25:16.673
In production-grade Agent systems, the vast majority of Harness code goes into these safeguards, not the context and tools alone.

540
01:25:16.673 --> 01:25:27.461
From Workflow to Autonomous Agent: Prompts first, then workflows, autonomous Agents last—that ordering is the most practical way to reduce unexpected behavior.

541
01:25:27.461 --> 01:25:33.748
Every orchestration pattern has situations where it fits; no single pattern is best everywhere.

542
01:25:33.748 --> 01:25:44.598
Five design patterns run through the book: Proposer-Reviewer, Progressive Disclosure, Append-only, Boundary Set + Retention Set, and Minimal Diff + Reversible.

543
01:25:44.598 --> 01:25:52.486
Security Is an Architectural Issue: Security has to be considered from the first line of code, not patched on before launch.

544
01:25:52.486 --> 01:26:01.786
Guardrails are divided by difficulty of bypass into context, execution, and data layers; all later security discussions use this structure.

545
01:26:01.948 --> 01:26:08.160
The next chapter examines the Harness's most central component in depth: context engineering.

546
01:26:08.110 --> 01:26:16.485
Chapter 8 covers the Agent concept's academic roots in reinforcement learning and compares traditional RL with modern LLM Agents.

547
01:26:16.485 --> 01:26:21.560
The thought questions below explore the chapter's core concepts in greater depth.

548
01:26:21.560 --> 01:26:25.110
There is no single correct answer to each question.

549
01:26:25.110 --> 01:26:27.048
Thought Questions.

550
01:26:27.048 --> 01:26:38.373
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  If you could only add one capability to an Agent system—a stronger model, richer context, or more tools—which would you choose?

551
01:26:38.373 --> 01:26:41.410
Under what conditions would your choice change?

552
01:26:41.410 --> 01:26:50.360
advanced difficulty, three stars:  In a ReAct loop, cumulative cache reads grow approximately quadratically with the number of rounds.

553
01:26:50.360 --> 01:26:52.898
How can this growth be reduced?

554
01:26:52.898 --> 01:27:01.998
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  The "Model as Agent" paradigm means models are becoming more autonomous in tool-calling decisions.

555
01:27:01.998 --> 01:27:07.873
However, this chapter argues that the importance of Harness engineering is actually increasing.

556
01:27:07.873 --> 01:27:10.823
How can these two trends coexist?

557
01:27:10.823 --> 01:27:14.660
Where does the future core value of Agent frameworks lie?

558
01:27:14.660 --> 01:27:25.673
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  In the ablation experiment, the absence of "tool result feedback" makes the Agent retry until it exhausts its iteration budget.

559
01:27:25.673 --> 01:27:33.348
In a production environment, besides missing tool results, what other situations could push an Agent into this kind of loop?

560
01:27:33.348 --> 01:27:37.210
What detection and termination mechanisms would you design?

561
01:27:37.210 --> 01:27:48.085
introductory difficulty, one star:  This chapter analyzed five Agent products along three dimensions: working context, action interfaces, and strategy.

562
01:27:48.085 --> 01:27:56.298
Pick an AI product you use daily, analyze it along the same three dimensions, and judge whether its architecture is appropriate.

563
01:27:56.298 --> 01:27:59.510
If you were designing it, what would you improve?

564
01:27:59.510 --> 01:28:10.085
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  If you were to design a customer service system specifically for booking flights, would you choose a workflow pattern or an autonomous Agent pattern?

565
01:28:10.085 --> 01:28:13.885
Is it possible to mix both patterns in the same system?

566
01:28:13.885 --> 01:28:20.098
advanced difficulty, three stars:  The guardrails section mentioned tool risk ratings.

567
01:28:20.098 --> 01:28:33.335
If a tool is generally low-risk but becomes high-risk with specific parameter combinations (e.g., delete_file deleting a normal file vs. deleting a system file), how would you design dynamic risk assessment?

568
01:28:33.335 --> 01:28:41.760
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  In the Agent product table in this chapter, all Agents have an "open-ended" action space.

569
01:28:41.760 --> 01:28:50.935
In what scenarios would a constrained action space (e.g., only being able to choose from predefined options) be superior to an open-ended one?

570
01:28:50.935 --> 01:28:59.548
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  The human-in-the-loop intervention mechanism requires the Agent to "gracefully hand over control.

571
01:28:59.548 --> 01:29:06.785
However, in practice, the user might be offline, respond slowly, or give vague instructions.

572
01:29:06.785 --> 01:29:09.748
What should the Agent do in such cases?

573
01:29:09.748 --> 01:29:24.560
advanced difficulty, three stars:  The introduction states that "good design principles should transcend model iteration cycles," but the concrete engineering methods used to implement those principles may become obsolete as model capabilities improve.

574
01:29:24.560 --> 01:29:29.110
Give an example of such an Agent engineering method and explain why.

